; A run writes a header, the output and a footer into a buffer of its
; own, in runs:mode, silent and unmodified, and ended entries see its end.
> (define b (runs:start-for-user "*run*" "printf 'one\\ntwo\\n'" home))
> (ended? b)
0
> (show b)
Run started at TIME in ~/

printf 'one\ntwo\n'
one
two

Run finished at TIME, after S s
> (list (modes:major-mode b) (buffers:silent? b) (buffers:modified? b) (runs:run-buffer? b) (runs:command b))
(runs:mode #t #f #t "printf 'one\\ntwo\\n'")
> (taken-ends)
(("*run*" 0 0))
> (message)
"*run* finished"
